---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import PageIntro from "../components/PageIntro.astro";
import data from "../data/foods.json";
import { TEASPOON_GL } from "../lib/foods";
---

<Layout
  title="About carbs are sugar: who runs it and how the numbers are made"
  description="Who runs carbsaresugar.com, why it exists, how its sugar teaspoon figures are produced, and what the site does not do: no products, no affiliate links, no data collected."
>
  <PageIntro
    slot="intro"
    title="About this site"
    lede="A free calculator that turns portions of food into teaspoons of table sugar, built on published research and nothing else."
  />

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 py-10 sm:px-6 sm:py-14">
    <div class="prose-body">
      <h2 style="margin-top:0">Who runs it</h2>
      <p>
        carbsaresugar.com is a small, independent reference site, built and maintained by one person, not a company or an
        editorial team. It answers one question and shows exactly where its numbers come from. The site is not affiliated with or endorsed by Dr
        David Unwin, the Public Health Collaboration, the Journal of Insulin Resistance, or any food, pharmaceutical or diet
        company.
      </p>

      <h2>Why it exists</h2>
      <p>
        Dr David Unwin's sugar infographics, published by the Public Health Collaboration at
        <a href="https://phcuk.org/sugar">phcuk.org/sugar</a>, are among the most widely shared nutrition charts in the world. They are clear and
        they are free, and they are also static images: a chart for bread, a chart for cereals, a chart for fruit. Anyone who
        wants to know the figure for their own breakfast, or their own day, has to find the right charts and add the numbers
        up by hand.
      </p>
      <p>
        This site does the adding. Pick the foods and portions, and it totals them in the same unit Dr Unwin uses: 4g
        teaspoons of table sugar with an equivalent effect on blood glucose. The figures sit beside one other number, the
        amount of glucose in an adult's bloodstream at any moment, and the reader takes it from there.
      </p>

      <h2>How the numbers are produced</h2>
      <p>
        The calculator currently lists {data.foods.length} foods. Almost all of the figures are copied directly from Dr
        Unwin's charts, distributed by the Public Health Collaboration at <a href="https://phcuk.org/sugar">phcuk.org/sugar</a>
        and used with PHC's written permission, or from Table 1 of the 2016 paper that sets out the
        method. A food's teaspoon figure is its glycaemic load divided by {TEASPOON_GL}, which is the glycaemic load of one 4g
        teaspoon of table sugar. The page <a href="/what-is-glycemic-load/">what is glycaemic load</a> shows that arithmetic
        step by step.
      </p>
      <p>
        The rules for the data are strict. No figure is estimated to fill a gap. Every food records its source and whether
        its figure was read from a chart, taken from a published table or calculated, and calculated figures record their
        inputs. Where two charts disagree, both numbers are recorded. Foods without a defensible published figure are left
        out. The full record is on the <a href="/sources/">sources page</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Twice a year, an automated check confirms that every source is still online and flags any change to the Public
        Health Collaboration's sugar page for a person to review. It does not change any values by itself.
      </p>

      <h2>What this site does not do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>No products are sold.</strong> There is nothing to buy here, and no paid tier.</li>
        <li><strong>No affiliate links.</strong> No link on this site earns a commission.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>No data is collected by the site.</strong> There are no accounts, no forms, no analytics and no database.
          Your food list lives in the page address and nowhere else. The site does carry Google AdSense advertising, and
          Google's own handling of data is described on the <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> The figures are educational estimates based on averages. They do not
          describe any individual's blood glucose, and they are not a basis for changing medication or diet. Anyone
          managing diabetes or another health condition should talk to a doctor, nurse or dietitian.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No food recommendations.</strong> The site shows figures. It does not say what anyone should or should not
          eat.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How it is paid for</h2>
      <p>
        Hosting costs are covered by display advertising served through Google AdSense. Advertisers have no say over the
        content or the data, and no food is included, excluded or described differently for commercial reasons.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        The site's credibility depends on its numbers being right. If a figure does not match its source, or a food is
        described wrongly, please use the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>. Corrections that include a source are the
        most useful mail this site receives, and they are acted on.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
